From: Jordan Bettcher <thebettcher@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ppc64le: build for POWER8, not POWER9

Applies on top of 0001 and only when _power8_compat=1, i.e. for the
omarchy-ppc64le baseline pool. The POWER9 pool keeps 0001's power9 floor.

bun has no ppc64le entry in cpuTargetFlags upstream; patch 0001 adds one and
pins it to POWER9 ("the only ppc64le hardware bun is built/tested on"). That
pin wins over makepkg's environment: CFLAGS/CXXFLAGS/RUSTFLAGS were correctly
-mcpu=power8 / -C target-cpu=pwr8 from tools/makepkg.conf.d/00-power8.conf and
the drop-in's own asserts passed, but bun computes its own flags from this
table and overrides all of them.

Measured on bun 1.4.3-4 built in the baseline lane: 349,172 ISA 3.0
instructions by tools/isa30-pkgscan.sh, against 347,558 for the POWER9 build --
the two were the same binary, ISA-wise. stxv 114906, lxv 93936, stxvx 47608,
lxvx 29288, vextubrx 18087, xxspltib 6370, cnttzd 4137, extswsli 3144,
maddld 2576, mtvsrdd 2274; spread uniformly over 62 of the 60.3 MiB of .text
and present in function prologues as VSX callee-save. Not a jump-table
artifact, not runtime-gated, and not the LLInt -- an offlineasm-generated
interpreter would be one contiguous block.

diff --git a/scripts/build/flags.ts b/scripts/build/flags.ts
index 76fc5f4b..98246883 100644
--- a/scripts/build/flags.ts
+++ b/scripts/build/flags.ts
@@ -73,11 +73,20 @@ export const cpuTargetFlags: Flag[] = [
     desc: "x64: Nehalem (2008) — no AVX, broadest compatibility",
   },
   {
-    // POWER9 is the floor: the only ppc64le hardware bun is built/tested on.
-    // POWER8 would also work (VSX-2), but nothing has exercised it.
-    flag: ["-mcpu=power9", "-mtune=power9"],
+    // POWER8 (ISA 2.07) is the floor for the baseline pool, which has to run on
+    // POWER8 through POWER11. This one entry decides all three halves of the
+    // binary, which is why it is the only thing this patch touches:
+    //   - the C++ sources, via computeCpuTargetFlags() into globalFlags;
+    //   - the Rust half, because rustCpuTargetFlags() in rust.ts derives
+    //     -Ctarget-cpu/-Ztune-cpu from this table, and LLVM_CPU_ALIASES already
+    //     maps power8 -> pwr8;
+    //   - the vendored WebKit, which inherits -mcpu through
+    //     computeCpuTargetFlags() and whose JavaScriptCore/CMakeLists.txt reads
+    //     it to set ENABLE_PPC64_ISA30, so offlineasm gets --ppc64-isa=power8
+    //     and the generated LLInt holds no ISA 3.0 either.
+    flag: ["-mcpu=power8", "-mtune=power8"],
     when: c => c.ppc64le,
-    desc: "ppc64le: POWER9 (VSX-3, little-endian ELFv2)",
+    desc: "ppc64le: POWER8 (VSX-2, little-endian ELFv2)",
   },
 ];
 
